WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980
Listen, I've got a test for you.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:05.040
I'm going to set up a fake scene
and see if you can tell

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.140
—that I am...
—In front of a green screen.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.940
Yeah. He is.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:16.980
But here's my question.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.060
When is AI going to replace this
clunky 20th century invention?

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.580
I mean, after all, AI is
the technology

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.480
that can draw masterpieces
and generate poems...

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.060
and hunt down our loved ones
and kill them...

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.180
so it can totally
beat out a color.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.200
Right?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.360
Right???

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.700
Okay.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.580
I found a fantastic picture
of a happy family.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.240
I am going to get rid of their background
to show you some of the ways

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.160
that we have taught AI
how to do it.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.820
I can start by just separating
the foreground

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.600
from the background.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.020
A lot of fast AI programs
do it this way

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.640
or it can do segmentation...

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.800
where I figure out and label

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.200
each part of the picture and
remove what I want.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.000
If I get even more
fine grained

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.820
I can label each instance
in a segment...

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.120
like Sheila, Lorraine,
and Sheila Junior...

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.760
and then get rid of the wall
and the floor.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.380
And Sheila Junior.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.820
She knows what she did.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.240
AI has gotten really good at

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.580
splitting stuff up,
at segmentation.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.560
For example, Meta AI

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.960
just released a new segmentation model

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.040
that can do this...

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.020
really easily!

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.240
An AI will use different methods

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.220
depending on the job
that it's told to do.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.540
But it's a bit different

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.580
when you're trying to make
a really good matte...

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.560
a perfect cut out.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.280
So you can add whatever
background you want.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.840
This is really hard stuff.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.920
See the hair?

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.780
That's often the key obstacle.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.240
Today, a lot of AIs basically

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.660
train off people doing what
I am doing now...

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:52.980
some labeling people

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.740
but also extremely tedious
separations

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.840
of the foreground from
the background.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.260
For one dataset, people at Adobe
took pictures like this

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.740
went through just like me
and made a perfect matte

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.680
You can see they left out
some really fine hair here.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.280
AII then looks at these matte and
learns how to create one.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.800
They might get more practice
by putting their matte over

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.160
different backgrounds and having it try
to cut out the person

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.540
again and again.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.740
I just want to be clear here

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.020
the way AI learned
how to do this

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.720
because some people were squinting
at their computer...

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.680
erasing backgrounds and
it learned to copy that.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.980
Others have expanded
the stuff AI can learn from

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.080
by taking greenscreen footage.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.480
They manually keyed out the footage

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:40.740
removed the green...

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.140
and then the model practiced

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.480
with those mattes on
different backgrounds...

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.540
trying to cut them out
over and over again

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.280
until it got really good.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.220
That helps the robot
improve its skills.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.780
Researcher Roni Sengupta and
his coauthors made this dataset.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.800
They used that data to make a really,
really, really good green screen.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.680
And then another one with
some researchers from Bytedance

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.060
the owners of TikTok.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.160
I am going to
pull it up now.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.400
It is like a Zoom background
on steroids.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.340
I especially had to instruct
all my subjects

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.760
to play with the hair because
there is no other part

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.640
where this transparency
becomes more visible

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.680
than the hair and the glasses.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.560
So I'm looking at people's faces
like this when they turn...

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.360
The glasses comes into effect.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.680
There is transparency.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.980
So in practice you are trying
to predict something

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.160
which is mostly zero in most part
of the background...

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.280
and it's mostly one at
the center of my body

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.700
but around my age and the hair
and everything...

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.779
It has some values that is
between zero and one.

00:03:46.779 --> 00:03:46.800
Between zero and one.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.680
He's talking about where the AI
has trouble predicting

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.080
what is Roni and
what is office.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.020
And that's a hard problem
to solve.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.240
So AI green screens
are great, right?

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.800
You don't need a big
green screen.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.080
But they fail in other ways.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.500
This is TikTok.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.780
Let me put on the green screen effect.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.640
So it looks good.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.940
But do you see how it's
kind of clamping down

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.380
on my head right here?

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.640
That's because these AI green screens
are optimized for speed

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.420
not necessarily for Hollywood-level quality.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.280
It doesn't look bad at first

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.340
but then when you see
the comparison, you realize

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.420
exactly how much information
it's cutting out.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.040
In that fancy loft

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.620
I stood close to a really
textured background.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.840
Do you see how the
AI green screen

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.060
had trouble making
a perfect separation?

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.460
It got some of the numbers right

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.860
but it's confused about others.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.520
And the final problem is something
that traditional green screens can do

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.340
that AI green screens
have to adapt to.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.620
I'll put my laptop's here,
way in front of me.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.620
The green screen has no problem
getting rid of all the green

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.740
because it doesn't have any knowledge
of what's in the frame.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.280
But the TikTok green screen is not
a real green screen.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.380
It's trained to look for people.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.020
So it's going to cut out my computer...

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.360
even though I might want it
in the shot.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.360
That's a problem that traditional
green screens...

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.280
obviously don't have.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.520
Somebody has to tell the AI
what to do.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.020
A lot of advanced
AI video editors

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.600
do this by making masks instead.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.060
A mask is basically
an outline.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.940
So the computer has you
click the outline

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.800
and does its best to make a mask

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.200
and move it along t
hroughout the frame.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.760
It can be a car,
a person

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.640
an animal out,
an object.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.820
And so we train a model just to
be able to recognize that

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:42.900
in a very accurate way.
right?

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.460
You want a perfect copy?
Perfect match.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.380
Okay. So this is
Chris Valenzuela.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.840
He is the CEO of this very popular

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.540
AI-focused editor called Runway.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:54.480
Their green screen tool is
one way of solving this

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.740
AI green screen problem

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.260
by having you choose what
you want to keep...

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.660
rather than just removing
the background.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.700
Where I see artificial intelligence
being very helpful

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.640
for video and creative endeavors is that...

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.260
It's not an automation tool.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.720
It's not something that will just
replace end-to-end

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.500
the whole process.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.540
It will just help you reduce

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.240
and replace the boring aspects
of a process.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.980
We are seeing two kinds of AI

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.360
emerge currently

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.820
for any kind of filmmaking
visual effects things.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.280
One that is very
purpose driven where...

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:29.820
we want to give cinematographers
actual control over

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:30.840
what is going to happen.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.400
And another one

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.280
which we want random people
to post their videos

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.540
and create cool effects.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.140
We imagine green screens
and AI as switches.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.920
They make more sense on a graph
of speed and quality.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.160
TikTok’s might be here.
An editing program might go here.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.660
And a greenscreen could go here.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.940
Good preparation can improve quality...

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.940
but perfection in almost every case...

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.060
still requires an artist
to finish the job.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.520
Is the model 100% there yet?

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.960
Probably not, but it's 90%.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.140
And then 90% is significant enough...

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:07.260
to reduce the time of translating hours
into minutes.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.960
That means less time
squinting at screens...

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:12.240
and more time in the loft

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.680
you rented to make this video

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.420
or with your family.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:23.580
Must destroy Sheila Junior.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:24.120
Okay.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:25.260
Okay. One more thing.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.140
We get a slightly cooler
background than this?

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.880
Okay, that's slightly.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.680
So a couple of years ago.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.060
I mean, this video that's called

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.460
“The technology that's replacing
the green screen”

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.240
And that's true.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.600
It’s these giant screens that are often
hooked up to a game engine...

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.060
and they provide realistic lighting

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.100
and movement of the background.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.660
It's amazing stuff.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.220
But since that video

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.740
and as the technology has become
more widespread...

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:56.400
visual effects artists have been open
that often they end up

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.320
roto-ing even these images.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.700
What is roto-ing?

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.240
Basically, it means that they
replace the background.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.880
They meticulously trace out
the characters...

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:08.820
and replace the background once again.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.920
Even though they were supposed to
have it in-camera.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.420
Now, this doesn't always happen

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.100
and there are still huge benefits

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.260
to having accurate lighting on set.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.000
But the point is simple.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.720
If you put those amazing LED walls
on our little graph...

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.300
they would still be just short...

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.820
of the perfection that you can get
from an artist...

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.520
who has a lot of time
on their hands.

